#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120648} :activity/id #uuid "8734274c-15b6-589a-a77f-6fd025ab063a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049788 :topic/id #uuid "1420f0fa-c9b8-5cba-8bba-e14cbf53c041" :topic/math-academy-id 1776} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121026 :tutorial/content "Cramer's rule applies to square systems of linear equations with any number of variables. In this lesson, we will practice applying Cramer's rule to solve $3 \\times 3$ systems of equations.\n\nSuppose we have a square system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y+{a}_{13}z={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y+{a}_{23}z={b}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{31}x+{a}_{32}y+{a}_{33}z={b}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & {a}_{13} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & {a}_{23} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32} & {a}_{33}\\end{aligned}|$. \nThen Cramer's rule states that\n\n$$\n\\left< x,y,z \\right> = \\left\\langle  \\dfrac{D_x}{D}, \\dfrac{D_y}{D}, \\dfrac{D_z}{D} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nis the unique solution of the system, where\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {a}_{12} & {a}_{13} \\\\ {b}_{2} & {a}_{22} & {a}_{23} \\\\ {b}_{3} & {a}_{32} & {a}_{33}\\end{aligned}|\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f245bf99-3b45-5a27-9aca-aaee391628ed" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3079 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "620dc976-5ed3-54d8-a37a-7483259c65bb" :step/math-academy-id 16705 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120706}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119789 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119600 :question/id #uuid "41a8466d-8bc6-5811-a557-e5a4e840f51c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4577"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c540ac-3cb5-5e0e-9b7b-5e2ccf1615c5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342060}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"} :step/id #uuid "c34a23fc-50c9-5fb1-b89d-0a53b98720ed" :step/math-academy-id 16707 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119791 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119602 :question/id #uuid "e1eb00d6-77fe-5110-8095-a54e63981c09" :question/math-academy-id "e-4579"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7263e25b-117e-528c-b2ad-2624f325da35" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342063}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"} :step/id #uuid "bd4aa514-64cb-5bde-8bf4-8e5364299dc3" :step/math-academy-id 16711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121047 :tutorial/content "To demonstrate using Cramer's rule on a $3 \\times 3$ system, consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y+z=9 \\\\ 2y+3z=7 \\\\ -8x+y+6z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y+1z=9 \\\\ 0x+2y+3z=7 \\\\ -8x+1y+6z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start finding the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}| & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(12-3)-2(0+24)+(0+16) \\\\  & =36-48+16 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $D_x,$ $D_y,$ and $D_z.$\n\n- To find $D_x,$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the *first* column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}| & =9\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =9(12-3)-2(42-0)+(7-0) \\\\  & =81-84+7 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find $D_y,$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the *second* column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}| & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}|-9\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(42)-9(0+24)+(0+56) \\\\  & =168-216+56 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find $D_z,$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the *third* column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -8 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}|+9\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(-7)-2(0+56)+9(0+16) \\\\  & =-28-112+144 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩ & =⟨\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{4},\\frac{8}{4},\\frac{4}{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9d916943-4be4-554c-a4f5-51aef55e8254" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3241 :tutorial/title "A Demonstration of Cramer's Rule on a 3x3 Linear System"} :step/id #uuid "e9ce92e1-dc54-5375-b685-ec0fb5099a5c" :step/math-academy-id 17835 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120745}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120745 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119852 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119667 :question/id #uuid "33693f3b-713f-5ffc-bd4e-27ad7a1e0c7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6172"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9c2dc67-95ca-52f2-a292-32891000b76a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050129}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393348}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of Cramer's Rule"} :step/id #uuid "d5e91e09-6b2c-5a23-a95e-c5b4f8576a52" :step/math-academy-id 23061 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120649}}] :activity/title "Cramer’s Rule for 3x3 Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119998}]}
